  {
    "episode_id": 83146,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83146.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "It's hard to say. Four and a half months is a long time. Before election day, a lot can happen. There's so many issues both here at home, whether you're talking about border security, the ones that I hear people expressing concern the most about is, and that transcends the usual D versus R left versus right lines in the sand. People are very concerned about our open borders. People are very concerned about increasing crime. These are the things that they're living within dealing with and not just border towns or states, but in the rural Midwest, they're being affected by this. People are extremely concerned about Biden's destruction of Title IX and the fact that their daughters are now being confronted with the potential of having a boy, a biological boy in the bathroom or in the locker room or competing against them in sports. issues with education, the lack of school choice. There are so many different issues here at home that are affecting people in a very, very direct way. that I hope will motivate them to take action and inform how they vote. And then, of course, we are facing wars either directly or indirectly in many different regions of the world or potential for wars. We are closer to the possibility of nuclear Armageddon now more than ever. So both on the domestic front and on the the foreign policy front, our national security, we are seeing many reasons why President Biden should not be reelected, why the Democrats can't be allowed to stay in power.",
